@meta
id: 01-11
title: Reading the bottom of the screen
chapter: 01
type: lesson
skills: mode-awareness, cmdline, insert-a, esc
par: 40
minutes: 5
vscode: the status bar, which you have probably never read either
open: incident.md

@brief
The last line of a vim window is where the editor talks to you, and new
users ignore it for months. It holds three different things at different
moments: the mode indicator, the messages vim prints, and the command
line you are typing on.

There is one more signal worth learning today. Press `Ctrl-G` and vim
prints the file's name, how many lines it has, where you are in it, and
whether it has unsaved changes — shown as a `[Modified]` flag, or `[+]`
where vim is short of room. That flag is the answer to "did I save this?",
and it is always on screen.

incident.md needs two additions, and you are going to save in between so
you can watch the flag appear, clear, and appear again.

  1. The Impact line should end `queued for four hours`.
  2. Then write the file with `:w` and press `Ctrl-G`.
  3. The Status line should read `resolved, monitoring`.

Then save and quit.

@keys
| `Ctrl-G` | print the file name, position and modified flag |
| `[+]` | seen in the message: unsaved changes exist |
| `:w` | write — the flag clears |
| `$` `a` | end of line, append |

@hints
The Impact line is two down from the top, the Status line is two below
that. `$a` gets you appending at the end of either one.
--
After `Esc`, type `:w` and Enter. Then `Ctrl-G` and read the line it
prints — no `[+]`, because everything on disk matches what you are
looking at.
--
Now the Status line: `jj` then `$a`, type `, monitoring`, `Esc`. Press
`Ctrl-G` again and the flag is back. `:wq` clears it for good.

@solution
    j j $ a <space> hours <Esc> :w<Enter>
    j j $ a , <space> monitoring <Esc> :wq<Enter>

`Ctrl-G` is not a key you will press often once you trust the flag, but it
is the fastest way to answer three questions at once when you are lost:
which file am I in, how far through it am I, and have I saved.

The habit to build is glancing at the bottom left before you type
anything, not after something has gone wrong. `-- INSERT --` there means
your next keystroke is a letter in your file. Nothing there means your
next keystroke is a command. That one glance prevents most of the
accidents in your first month.

@start incident.md
# Payment incident 2026-05-11

Impact: refunds queued for four
Owner: priya
Status: resolved

@expected incident.md
# Payment incident 2026-05-11

Impact: refunds queued for four hours
Owner: priya
Status: resolved, monitoring
